    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the only test module, and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the daily-registration cap: creating an owner is now rejected with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100+ owners already share today's `registrationDate`.\n\nI kept structural impact low by adding the behaviour as a small, cohesive unit rather than growing any existing method or class, following the codebase's established policy pattern:\n\n1. **New file** `OwnerDailyLimitPolicy.java` \u2014 a self-contained policy with a single static `rejectWhenDailyLimitReached(...)` method (counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()`, throws `DailyLimitReachedException` at \u2265100) and its nested exception. No surrounding complexity to hold.\n\n2. **One line added** to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` \u2014 a single policy call slotted alongside the existing policy invocations (plus its import). The method's own complexity is unchanged.\n\n3. **One handler added** to `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2014 a small `@ExceptionHandler` mapping the exception to 429, mirroring the existing `CityAtCapacityException` handler (the `@ResponseStatus` annotation alone isn't honored because the catch-all `Exception` handler wins).\n\nThe 429 mapping via a dedicated advice handler was the one non-obvious requirement \u2014 the `@ResponseStatus(TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)` on the exception was overridden by the global `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)`, producing a 500 until the explicit handler was added. Full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
